<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#geez">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ge'ez</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ge'ez script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ge'ez is an ancient Semitic language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, with its own script. It was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>'s inscriptions and became the liturgical and literary language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and its church, flourishing under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geez.jpg" alt="Ge'ez" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aksum,_iscrizione_di_re_ezana,_in_greco,_sabeo_e_ge%27ez,_330-350_dc_ca._11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Geʽez, sometimes called Classical Ethiopic in scholarly writing, is an ancient South Semitic language from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. Most linguists believe that it is not the common ancestor of the modern Ethio-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> but separated early from another unattested common language. Inscriptions in Sabaic have been found in the kingdom of Dʿmt from the middle of the first millennium BCE, and Semitic languages have been present in Eritrea since about 2000 BCE. Geʽez is not regarded as an offshoot of Sabaic. The earliest Geʽez inscriptions, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>, date to as early as the 4th century, and the oldest known example of the script is on the Hawulti obelisk in Matara, Eritrea. It is unvocalised and contains pagan references.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Geʽez is written with the Ethiopic script, an abugida first developed for this language and called fidäl in languages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> that use it. It is read from left to right. The script has been adapted for other languages, chiefly Amharic and Tigrinya, and Tigre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. The language distinguishes two genders and two numbers, and nouns have an unmarked nominative and an accusative marked with final -a. The normal word order of a declarative sentence has the verb first. Because it is no longer spoken in daily life by large communities, the early pronunciation of some consonants is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Surviving Geʽez literature properly begins in the 4th century with the Christianization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">Ezana</a>. Almost all texts from the early Aksumite period are Christian and translated from Greek, producing an Ethiopic Bible of 81 books. The Book of Enoch survives complete in no other language. The oldest surviving Geʽez manuscript is thought to be the second of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garima-gospels">Garima Gospels</a>, dating to the 5th or 6th century. After the decline of Aksum, Geʽez was no longer a living language, replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> in the south and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> and Tigre in the north. It remained the official written language until the 19th century, in a position compared to that of Medieval Latin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>In the 14th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>, retelling the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and their son Menelik I, was written, and the first royal chronicles date to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a>. Literature flourished especially under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>, who himself wrote the Book of Light and the Book of the Nativity. The Fetha Negest, the Laws of the Kings, was translated into Geʽez around 1450 and served as supreme law until a modern constitution replaced it in 1931. Geʽez remains the liturgical language of the Ethiopian and Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo Churches and of the Beta Israel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geʽez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gold, silver and bronze issues, inscribed mainly in Greek and later also in <b>Ge'ez</b>, enabled Aksum to participate fully in the Mediterranean monetary economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He centralised administration by replacing hereditary regional lords with appointed officials, defeated the Adal Sultanate at the Battle of Gomit in 1445, and patronised a golden age of <b>Ge'ez</b> literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The term belongs to Amharic and Tigrinya and has no equivalent in <b>Ge'ez</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li></ul></section>
